key|name|url|evidence
country|OPEC Fund — Saudi Arabia country profile|https://opecfund.org/who-we-are/member-countries/saudi-arabia|Capital Riyadh; kingdom occupies most of Arabian Peninsula; borders listed. Use stable geography only, excluding changing economic statistics.
overview|Saudipedia — Saudi Arabia|https://saudipedia.com/en/saudi-arabia|Saudi riyal; 13 administrative provinces; western Red Sea and eastern Arabian Gulf; founder King Abdulaziz; unification 23 September 1932. Exclude disputed banknote and mining claims.
emblem|Saudipedia — Saudi national emblem|https://saudipedia.com/en/what-is-the-emblem-of-saudi-arabia|Two crossed swords beneath a palm tree. Swords symbolize strength, protection and sacrifice; palm symbolizes growth, prosperity and abundance.
nationalday|National Center for Non-Profit Sector — Saudi National Day|https://ncnp.gov.sa/en/media-center/saudi-national-day|23 September commemorates the Kingdom's unification in 1932 by King Abdulaziz.
founding|Saudipedia — Saudi Founding Day|https://saudipedia.com/en/saudi-founding-day|Annual observance 22 February commemorates establishment of first Saudi state in 1727 by Imam Mohammed bin Saud, with Diriyah its capital. Do not imply independently established exact historical founding day.
history|Saudipedia — History of Saudi Arabia|https://saudipedia.com/en/history-of-saudi-arabia|Mani bin Rabia al-Muraydi founded Diriyah in 1446; first Saudi state 1727–1818; second state established 1824 by Imam Turki bin Abdullah; King Abdulaziz recovered Riyadh in 1902; Kingdom unified 1932.
anthem|Saudipedia — Saudi National Anthem|https://saudipedia.com/en/saudi-national-anthem|Lyrics by Ibrahim Khafaji; Siraj Umar arranged the music to fit the existing Royal Salute; first broadcast 29 June 1984. Do not confuse arranger with original Royal Salute composer.
flag|Saudipedia — Saudi National Flag|https://saudipedia.com/en/the-saudi-national-flag|Green national flag bears white shahada and sword; Jeddah flagpole achieved a historical record in 2014. No claim to current global record.
holidays|Saudipedia — Official Holidays|https://saudipedia.com/en/official-holidays-in-saudi-arabia|Weekly weekend Friday and Saturday; changed from Thursday and Friday in June 2013 during King Abdullah's reign.
vision|Saudi Vision 2030 — Overview|https://www.vision2030.gov.sa/en/overview|Vision 2030 launched in 2016; national framework for economic and social development.
tourism|Saudi Tourism Authority — About Saudi|https://www.visitsaudi.com/en/about-saudi|Riyadh, Jeddah, AlUla and Aseer destination descriptions; Jeddah's Al-Balad and wooden roshans; AlUla's Elephant Rock and Hegra; Abha and Soudah in Aseer. Indexed text available; page HTML is client rendered.
hegra|UNESCO — Hegra Archaeological Site|https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1293|First Saudi World Heritage inscription in 2008; largest preserved Nabataean site south of Petra; monumental rock-cut tombs with decorated facades.
turaif|UNESCO — At-Turaif District in Diriyah|https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1329|First capital of Saudi dynasty; northwest of Riyadh; Najdi architectural style; inscription 2010.
ahsa|UNESCO — Al-Ahsa Oasis|https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1563|Cultural landscape inscribed 2018, with gardens, canals, springs, wells, historic buildings and archaeological sites; longstanding oasis settlement and water management.
hima|UNESCO — Hima Cultural Area|https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1619/|Ancient caravan route; rock art and inscriptions including Musnad, Aramaic-Nabataean, Thamudic, Greek and Arabic; Bir Hima wells still produce fresh water; inscription 2021.
jeddah|UNESCO — Historic Jeddah|https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1361/|Red Sea port and gateway for pilgrims to Makkah; coral-stone buildings and wooden roshan tower houses; Suez Canal opening in 1869 contributed to renewed trade. Exclude inconsistent century wording in caliph passage.
jareesh|Saudipedia — Jareesh|https://saudipedia.com/en/jareesh|Culinary Arts Commission designated jareesh the national dish in 2023; main ingredient coarsely crushed wheat.
cuisine|Saudipedia — Saudi Cuisine|https://saudipedia.com/en/saudi-cuisine|Maqshush designated national dessert; small wheat-flour pieces served with ghee and honey, sugar or date syrup; regional culinary distinctions. Avoid repetitive region-to-dish recall items.
astronauts|Saudipedia — Saudi astronauts|https://saudipedia.com/en/are-there-saudi-astronauts|Prince Sultan bin Salman flew aboard Discovery in June 1985; Rayyanah Barnawi and Ali al-Qarni traveled to ISS in May 2023; Barnawi first Saudi female astronaut.
masmak|Saudipedia — Al-Masmak Palace|https://saudipedia.com/en/al-masmak-palace|Riyadh landmark associated with recapture in 1902; thick fortified walls; clay construction with stone foundations; converted to ammunition storage after recapture. Exclude conflicting construction and museum inauguration dates.
coffee|Saudipedia — Saudi Coffee|https://saudipedia.com/en/saudi-coffee|Ministry of Culture designated 2022 Year of Saudi Coffee; naming initiative promoted Saudi coffee wording. Exclude coffee-origin speculation and shared Gulf hospitality facts.
aramco|Saudi Aramco — Company history|https://japan.aramco.com/en/about-us/aramco-overview/history-of-aramco|1933 oil concession to Standard Oil Company of California; commercial oil discovered at Dammam No. 7 in 1938.
causeway|Saudipedia — King Fahd Causeway|https://saudipedia.com/en/king-fahd-causeway|Links Saudi Arabia with Bahrain; Saudi end south of Al-Khobar; inaugurated by King Fahd and Sheikh Isa bin Salman in 1986. Passport Island consists of joined Saudi western and Bahraini eastern halves.
farasan|Saudipedia — Farasan Island|https://saudipedia.com/en/farasan-island|Farasan in Jazan Province, southwestern Saudi Arabia; archipelago in Red Sea. Exclude fluctuating distance and island-count estimates.
prophetmosque|Saudipedia — The Prophet's Mosque|https://saudipedia.com/en/the-prophet%22s-mosque|The Prophet's Mosque is in Madinah in western Saudi Arabia.
wadjda|Sony Pictures Classics — Wadjda acquisition press release|https://www.sonyclassics.com/downloads/Wadjda-Release.pdf|Film directed by Haifaa Al-Mansour; central girl's determined quest to buy a bicycle. Official distributor release.
argentina2022|FIFA — Saudi Arabia defeats Argentina at Qatar 2022|https://www.fifa.com/en/articles/world-cup-qatar-2022-argentina-v-saudi-arabia-match-report-review-first-game-group-e-result|Saudi Arabia won 2–1 at Lusail on 22 November 2022; Saleh Al-Shehri equalized; Salem Al-Dawsari scored winner.
hadi|World Athletics — Saudi Arabia's new prodigy, 6 October 2000|https://worldathletics.org/news/news/saudi-arabias-new-prodigy-1|Hadi Souan Al-Somayli won Sydney 2000 Olympic silver in men's 400 metres hurdles; Angelo Taylor Olympic champion.
alula|Saudipedia — AlUla|https://saudipedia.com/en/alula|Maraya is AlUla's venue hosting cultural and artistic events.
haramain|Saudipedia — Haramain High Speed Railway|https://saudipedia.com/en/haramain-high-speed-railway|Passenger railway links Makkah and Madinah, with stations in Jeddah and at King Abdulaziz International Airport.
uruq|UNESCO — Uruq Bani Maarid|https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1699/|Western edge of Empty Quarter; Arabian oryx reintroduction; longitudinal dune systems over limestone plateau and southern Tuwaiq escarpment; low-relief coarse-sand zibars lack well-formed slip faces. Natural World Heritage inscription 2023.
faw|UNESCO — Al-Faw Archaeological Area|https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1712/|Qaryat al-Faw capital of Kinda; important caravan relay between Najran and central/eastern Arabia; water depletion led to abandonment in fifth century CE; sacred mountain Khashm Qaryah; inscription 2024.
tuwaiq|Official Saudi Founding Day — English guide|https://foundingday.sa/en/-/media/FoundingDay/PDFs/Guides/EN/foundingday-book-guideline-en.pdf|Tuwaiq in central Najd; name diminutive of tawq or ring; historic caravan routes. Do not use inconsistent range-length estimates.
edge|Saudi Press Agency — Edge of the World, 7 May 2025|https://spa.gov.sa/en/N2313396|Edge of the World is landmark of Tuwaiq mountain range near Riyadh. Avoid inconsistent mountain-range length estimates.
ithra|Ithra — Official center website|https://www.ithra.com/en?trk=test|King Abdulaziz Center for World Culture, known as Ithra, is a Saudi Aramco initiative in Dhahran.
saudia|Saudipedia — Saudi Arabian Airlines|https://saudipedia.com/en/saudi-arabian-airlines-saudia|Saudia is Saudi Arabia's first airline; first flights in 1945. Exclude conflicting formal establishment dates.
abdu|Saudipedia — Mohammed Abdu|https://saudipedia.com/en/mohammed-abdu|Saudi singer and composer known as Artist of the Arabs and Abu Noura; composed and sang Fawq Ham Al-Sahab. Avoid copying the source's quiz wording.
sparks|International Prize for Arabic Fiction — 2010 winner|https://archive.arabicfiction.org/en/2010|Throwing Sparks by Saudi novelist Abdo Khal won the 2010 award.
maraya|Royal Commission for AlUla — Maraya brochure|https://www.experiencealula.com/content/dam/rcudxp/global/atm/Maraya%20Brochure.pdf|Maraya concert and event venue in AlUla; exterior fully clad in mirrors reflecting canyon landscape.
metro|Royal Commission for Riyadh City — Metro operation schedule|https://www.rcrc.gov.sa/en/rcrc-announces-sequential-operation-schedule-of-riyadh-metro-lines/|Six lines; Blue Line Al-Olaya–Al-Batha; Yellow Line King Khalid International Airport Road; Purple Line Abdulrahman bin Auf and Sheikh Hassan bin Hussein roads. Opening schedule 2024. Do not use changing station counts.
